{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_56","type":"agent_message","text":"The July send is **not ready**. The link reaches the judge in the recorded dry run, but the launcher can exceed $0.15 before it stops, and the dry run can pass without proving that each judge read the right prior answer.\n\n## Blocking findings\n\n1. **The $0.15 limit is enforced against estimates.** `SharedLedger.reserve` checks the estimate before sending. `settle` adds the reported charge and flags an overrun after the call. I exercised those methods in memory: with $0.140 already spent, a $0.008 reservation was granted, a $0.012 settlement took the total to $0.152, and the ledger then stopped. Before sending, establish a verified upper bound for each Luna attempt and for the full Jev retry bundle, and reserve against those bounds. If those bounds cannot be established, the current estimate-and-stop behavior needs Alex’s approval as a change to D694’s cap. Price the rebuilt linked requests as well. The recorded $0.0586 estimate covers six copies of the stored judge calls and omits the new Jev calls and the possible extra July 11 Data Center call. [D694](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/DECISIONS-log.md:10439) · [Budget reservation and settlement](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/clean-arch/e2e-comparison-2026-11-13/budget_replay.py:131) · [July price calculation](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:342)\n\n2. **The dry run can accept the wrong prior answer.** Its assertion accepts any matching sequence label anywhere after the previous-conclusion heading. An older answer or the other question’s answer can satisfy it, and control and linked runs reuse the same labels. The dependency assertion checks only for a heading. Give each fake answer a distinct marker for its sequence, question and attempt. Check the exact prior accepted answer in each question’s previous-conclusion block and that day’s accepted v14 comparison point and forecast in v9’s dependency block. Make freshness an enforced condition for comparison. The current summary records a boolean that also passes on zero judgements, and `send` never checks it. [Dry-run assertions](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:214) · [Freshness summary](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:150) · [Test plan](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/TEST-PLAN.md:20)\n\n3. **A refusal needs to stop new attempts and leave a clear record.** The item runner inspects seq 148 before it yields, as designed. Its test would still pass if a judge had been called on July 10, because it only rules out committed judgements on later days. Also, daily judging runs concurrently. `JudgingScope` raises on a disallowed request, but another worker can still enter the budget transport after that refusal. Make the refusal latch for that sequence, allowing already-started attempts to settle, and exercise both paths through replay with a recording transport. For the out-of-pair case, assert zero judge attempts and zero later item handovers. Record each sequence as it starts and finishes, including stops and unstarted sequences. Today a non-budget stop can leave the overall result `complete`, and a setup exception or interruption can leave no final summary. The existing directory checks and both ordinary launchers’ refusals do prevent resuming the same scratch store through the supported entry points. [July scopes and item runner](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:56) · [Out-of-pair assertion](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:244) · [Concurrent judging](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:334) · [Send summary](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:375)\n\n4. **The paid launcher does not verify the starting state used in the proof.** `prepare` accepts a supplied store if its maximum completed sequence is 147 and it has no later processing steps. Those checks do not establish the July 9 date or the contents of the proved old-2 snapshot. Verify the copied snapshot against the vetted rebuild before enabling transport. Add the required preflight output to `PROOF.md`, including skipped rows, and record the inspection of the rebuilt requests at the transport boundary. The prefix logs establish equality on the fields their comparison selected. [Prefix admission](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:116) · [Rebuild comparison](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/rebuild_control.py:37) · [AGENTS §5](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/AGENTS.md:180)\n\n5. **A later Jev failure loses earlier answers from the selection record.** I reproduced this with three jobs: the first answered and was archived, the second failed, and the third never ran. The `failed` record listed all three hashes, attributed no call ID to the first answer, and did not identify the second hash as the failure. Record each job and its answer as it completes, then attach the failing job’s exact hash. Extend the single-job failure test to cover success followed by failure with a third job still pending. [Selection code](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/links.py:178) · [Design §1 step 6](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/DESIGN.md:51) · [Current failure test](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/tests/test_links.py:400)\n\n## Verified and nonblocking\n\n- **Archive and filing.** In memory, the exact old v9, v14 and v8 requests initially hit their original call IDs. After retiring the 145 target rows, v9 and v14 went to the fake transport, v8 kept its original call ID, and a second retirement forced another v9 call. That verifies archive retirement. The checked-in archive proof calls `retire` directly, so describe it as an archive test. I also reran the filing step on the 105 stored statements with both links. All 105 kept both links with `kind='reads'`, and zero kept both when the same row was treated as a parent. That is a filing-step result on stored answers. [Archive proof](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/july_test.py:302) · [Filing step](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/extraction.py:468) · [Filing census](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/filing-exposure.md:3)\n- **Writer and compatibility.** Selection checks the whole proposed graph, and the writer checks one insertion at a time inside the savepoint. A cycle completed by a later insertion rolls back the whole write. Align §4’s description with that implementation and add a test for rollback after an earlier insertion. The v7 renderer still takes its legacy branch, `research_context` preserves its dependency columns, and the reading-rule hash matches `PROOF.md`. I reviewed the suite and mutant records without rerunning the suite. [Writer savepoint](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/writing.py:113) · [Judging renderer](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/views/judging.py:154) · [Research context](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/src/hivemind/research_context.py:34) · [Proof record](/Users/lchingen/hivemind/docs/research/2026-11-20-production-readiness/dependency-design/PROOF.md:10)\n\n**NOT READY**"}}
